# Saturn Explorer - Live Debugging walkthrough (~4-6 min). Needs a LIVE, patched emulator.
# Connect first (Session > Source > Connect to Emulator) and get the game to a spot where
# something interesting happens on a known action (a line of dialogue, a menu, a hit).
# This is an operator-driven walkthrough: the script sequences the panels and the narration
# tells the story, while YOU perform the clicks (set a breakpoint, create a tracepoint). Use
# manual mode (F8) so you can take as long as you need on each beat.

@beat intro hold 9
  note "Saturn Explorer isn't just a viewer - connected to a patched emulator, it's a live debugger built for reverse-engineers. Here's the SH-2 Assembly panel, disassembling around the CPU's program counter in real time as the game runs."
  solo assembly
  focus assembly

@beat breakpoints hold 12
  note "Click in the gutter to set a breakpoint, exactly like Visual Studio. The emulator halts the instant the CPU reaches that address. From here you get Run to Here, and step into, over, and out - full instruction-level control of a running Saturn game."
  solo assembly breakpoints
  focus breakpoints

@beat callstack hold 11
  note "The moment it breaks, the Call Stack panel reconstructs how the code got here - a confirmed stack when the emulator provides one, or a heuristic walk when it doesn't. Click any frame and the Assembly and Memory panels jump to it. This is the paused-state workspace."
  solo callStack assembly
  focus callStack

@beat tracepoints-open hold 13
  note "But breakpoints only let you STOP. What reverse-engineers really want is to WATCH. That's a tracepoint - a non-halting action attached to an address. Open the Tracepoints panel and create one, with a format string like: Dialogue ID equals, open brace r4. When the CPU hits that address, it logs the value and keeps running."
  solo actions assembly
  focus actions

@beat tracepoints-log hold 14
  note "Now let the game play. Every time execution crosses that address, the tracepoint fires and writes a line into the structured Log - the game narrating itself. You can even dereference pointers, like reading a character's name straight out of memory as a string, with no emulator-side code at all. The Log is filterable by category and every entry jumps back to its instruction."
  solo log actions
  focus log

@beat memory hold 11
  note "Alongside that: the Watch panel evaluates expressions and memory reads every frame; the Memory hex editor views and edits work RAM live; and RAM Search scans for a value the way Cheat Engine does - change it in-game, scan again, and narrow millions of addresses down to the one you want."
  solo watch hexEditor ramSearch
  focus watch

@beat outro hold 8
  note "Breakpoints, a reconstructed call stack, tracepoints that make a game explain itself, and live memory tools - Saturn Explorer turns a running game into something you can read. Combine this with the graphics tools, and you can follow a sprite from the screen all the way back to the code that drew it."
  solo assembly log
